    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "h86rebz",
      "content": "• Hydration – absorption of water causing expansion."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "dusq805",
      "content": "• Solution – complete dissolution of soluble minerals."
    },
    {
      "id": "8sll7nw",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "D. Special Forms of Weathering"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "8772rz5",
      "content": "• Spheroidal Weathering – rounded rock forms due to chemical attack at corners."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "cldme02",
      "content": "• Laterization – intense leaching in tropical climates forming laterite soils."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "16xu910",
      "content": "• Differential Weathering – softer rocks weather faster than hard rocks."
    },
    {
      "id": "3swkry7",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "E. Factors Affecting Weathering"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "8xgzqmw",
      "content": "• Climate (temperature &amp; rainfall)"
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "Differentiate between disintegration (mechanical weathering) and decomposition (chemical weathering) for conceptual MCQs.",
        "Remember that chemical weathering is most intense in hot and humid climates, whereas mechanical weathering dominates in arid and freeze-thaw environments.",
        "Pay attention to special weathering processes like hydration, oxidation, carbonation, and frost action as direct question sources."
      ],
      "id": "7yca28p"
    },
